    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/1/81237.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 54,
    "turn_text": "Well, there's this idea that you can't have a strong economy and a healthy environment at the same time. And I would argue that you look at the cleanest, safest, healthiest environments in the world and it's where you have the strongest economies. And if we move into a position where inflation's increasing, which means people's money has less purchasing power, where they're focused more on the basic needs, there's gonna be less money to invest in new technologies and new practices. So what we have to do is figure out how to make the economy and the environment work together and it gets into what I would call market-based conservation. That's how we can solve the problem. If you look at, you know, people have to eat. People have to keep warm. And if it gets down to, you know, killing an animal that you have to have for food or conserving that population of animals, people are going to kill the animal. It gets down to clearing trees so you can plant food to eat or starving. You're going to cut down the tree and plant the crop. If you have to have to burn to keep warm, you're going to burn the wood to keep warm.",
